diagnosis_text:
I could not reproduce the issue. Layer-3 connectivity between PC4 (172.16.10.4) and Laptop0 (172.16.20.10) is working normally now. Direct tests show 0% loss in both directions with low RTT: PC4→Laptop0 (1.03–1.86 ms) and Laptop0→PC4 (1.28–2.14 ms). Both hosts have correct addressing and default gateways (PC4 via 172.16.10.1, Laptop0 via 172.16.20.1).

The inter-floor path (Switch1 ↔ MSwitch1 ↔ Router0) also looks healthy. Trunk links are up on both ends (ethtool reports “Link detected: yes” on switch1 eth0 and mswitch1 eth0). VLAN segmentation is consistent: on Switch1, PC4’s port (eth1) is in br_vlan10 and Laptop0’s port (eth7) is in br_vlan20, with trunk subinterfaces eth0.10 and eth0.20 present. On MSwitch1, br_vlan10 and br_vlan20 are present; trunks eth0.10/20 (to Switch1) and eth4.10/20 (to Router0) are up, which supports inter-VLAN routing via Router0’s .1 gateways.

Mechanism-wise, if there had been a transient flap or ARP/bridge table timeout on the trunk or SVI path, it could briefly disrupt inter-floor reachability while same-floor traffic stayed fine. However, at this time there is no evidence of a persistent fault.

Root cause: Not reproducible; likely a transient condition that self-cleared.

Corrective fix: No action required now. Recommend monitoring for recurrences and, if it happens again, capture timestamps and check Switch1/MSwitch1 trunk counters and Router0 subinterface logs for flaps or MAC/ARP anomalies. === END ===